        <item>
            <title>0.15.2</title>
            <pubDate>Wed, 16 Sep 2026 20:55:24 +0200</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>1502</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.15.2</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>14.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Jarvis 0.15.2</h2>
<p><b>Take a task with you.</b> Everything a task knows — the ask, who made it,
what the deadline is, the messages it came out of — could be read on screen but
never carried anywhere. Now one press copies the lot.</p>

<h3>Copy a task and its context</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Open a task and the top bar carries a copy button, beside the owl. It puts
      the whole task on the clipboard as plain Markdown: the title, who asked and
      when it is due, why it is a task, the next step, and every message it was
      read out of — each under its conversation, with the link back.</li>
  <li>Paste it into a coding agent, another assistant, a document or a message to
      a colleague. Nothing has to be retyped.</li>
  <li>A citation Jarvis cannot read right now — offline, outside the
      conversations you chose, or rate limited — copies as its timestamp and
      link, exactly as the screen shows it.</li>
</ul>

<p>No change to what leaves your Mac: Slack is still searched live and never
stored, and mail and meetings stay on your machine. Copying writes nothing to
disk — the words go to your clipboard because you asked for them.</p>
]]></description>
