        <item>
            <title>0.15.1</title>
            <pubDate>Tue, 15 Sep 2026 17:38:38 +0200</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>1501</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.15.1</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>14.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Jarvis 0.15.1</h2>
<p><b>A task Jarvis found, and then quietly dropped.</b> Two fixes: one recovers
work that was being lost between deciding and telling you, the other makes the
app say what it can be clicked.</p>

<h3>Tasks that were found and then lost</h3>
<ul>
  <li>When Jarvis weighed a message, decided it was yours, and then failed to
      list it, nothing caught the contradiction — the task simply never
      appeared, with no sign anywhere that it had been found. Jarvis now
      notices and asks itself again before finishing the run.</li>
  <li>Someone naming you in a channel is choosing you out of everyone there, so
      a direct mention that asks a question, reports a problem, or requests
      something now counts as yours unless the conversation shows it was
      already answered.</li>
  <li>A task you already marked done is never reopened by this.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The pointer</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Everything clickable now shows the hand cursor — your tasks on the day,
      the messages behind a task, the buttons in a task, and controls across
      the Hub, meetings, settings and the notes log.</li>
  <li>A control with nothing to open keeps the plain arrow, so the hand always
      means a click will do something.</li>
</ul>

<p>No change to what leaves your Mac: Slack is still searched live and never
stored, and mail and meetings stay on your machine.</p>
]]></description>
